# ERP Unite — under the hood

The architecture, the patterns, the trade-offs. For HSO's technical architects.

<div class="byline">Technical architect pack · .NET 10 · Blazor · CQRS · EF Core</div>

---

<!-- _class: stat -->

<div class="number">strict layering</div>

## the architecture's load-bearing decision

UI → View Services → CQRS handlers → Repositories/UoW → DbContext. **No shortcuts. Boundaries enforced by review, not by reflection.**

---

# The layer model

Four layers. Each owns one thing. Dependencies flow inward.

<div class="cols two">
<div class="card">

### **Presentation**
Blazor Server components. Razor markup + code-behind. Inject view services only — never repositories, never `DbContext`, never handlers.

</div>
<div class="card">

### **Application**
CQRS handlers. Query handlers use `IDbContextFactory<UniteHubCoreDbContext>` + `AsNoTracking`. Command handlers use `IUnitOfWork` for transactional writes.

</div>
</div>

<div class="cols two">
<div class="card">

### **Persistence**
EF Core. `GenericRepository<T>` base with dual constructors (factory + context). `IUnitOfWork` aggregates repositories for transactions.

</div>
<div class="card">

### **Domain**
Entities + enums. No business logic — that lives in command handlers and application services. Domain is the schema, not the behavior.

</div>
</div>

*Net: every change has a clear destination layer. No more "where do I put this".*

---

# CQRS without MediatR

Deliberately simple. No mediator pipeline. No reflection-based dispatch.

- **`ICommandHandler<TCommand, TResult>`** — explicit interface, explicit registration
- **`IQueryHandler<TQuery, TResult>`** — same shape on the read side
- **Handlers injected directly** into view services or other handlers
- **Trade-off accepted:** ~200 DI registrations in `ServiceExtensions.cs` per hub
- **Trade-off rejected:** the implicit-magic cost of MediatR — debuggers can step through every call

*Net: handlers are debuggable. Stack traces are readable. Newcomers find code by name.*

---

# Read path vs write path

The two are not the same. The architecture treats them differently on purpose.

```text
READ PATH                          WRITE PATH
─────────────                      ─────────────
Component                          Component
  ↓ inject                           ↓ inject
ViewService                        ViewService
  ↓ calls                            ↓ calls
QueryHandler                       CommandHandler
  ↓ uses                             ↓ uses
IDbContextFactory                  IUnitOfWork
  ↓ new context per call             ↓ shared context per transaction
AsNoTracking() + .Select(...)      Repositories via UoW.GetRepository<T>()
  ↓                                  ↓
DTO projection                     SaveChanges + Commit/Rollback
```

*Net: reads stay fast and side-effect-free; writes stay transactionally safe.*

---

<!-- _class: section -->

# The Unit of Work

Why it exists. How it works. When to use it.

---

# Unit of Work — the transaction boundary

For multi-step writes, the Unit of Work makes "all-or-nothing" trivial.

- **Scoped lifetime** — one UoW per request/circuit
- **`BeginTransactionAsync` / `CommitAsync` / `RollbackAsync`** — explicit boundary
- **`GetRepository<TConcrete>()`** — concrete type, not interface, by design
- **Shared `DbContext`** across all repositories in the UoW
- **Save once** at the end — change tracking does the work

```csharp
await using var uow = unitOfWorkFactory.Create();
await uow.BeginTransactionAsync();
try {
    var partyRepo = uow.GetRepository<PartyRepository>();
    var roleRepo  = uow.GetRepository<UserRoleRepository>();
    // ... mutations ...
    await uow.SaveChangesAsync();
    await uow.CommitAsync();
} catch { await uow.RollbackAsync(); throw; }
```

*Net: complex writes look like simple code.*

---

# Repository pattern — the "boring on purpose" layer

Repositories are deliberately dumb. Anything fancy goes to a query handler.

- **`GenericRepository<T>`** base — `ReadByIdAsync`, `ReadAsync(predicate)`, `ListAsync`, `AddAsync`, `UpdateAsync`, `DeleteAsync`
- **Dual constructors** — `IDbContextFactory` (DI mode) + `UniteHubCoreDbContext` (UoW mode)
- **`ExecuteWithContextAsync`** helper — uses UoW context if present, otherwise creates short-lived
- **No `IQueryable` leakage** — materialize inside the repo
- **No DTO projection** — that's the query handler's job
- **No business logic** — that's the command handler's job

*Net: 90+ repositories. Each one is 30-80 lines. Boring. Reusable. Replaceable.*

---

<!-- _class: section -->
<!-- _color: #00B294 -->

# The DI shape

How handlers wire up. Why it scales.

---

# Composition root — one big registration

DI registration lives in each hub's `ServiceExtensions.cs`. ~200 lines per hub.

```csharp
services.AddDbContextFactory<UniteHubCoreDbContext>(opts => opts.UseSqlServer(conn));
services.AddScoped<IUnitOfWork, UnitOfWork>();
services.AddScoped<IPartyRepository, PartyRepository>();

services.AddScoped<ICommandHandler<SplitCashApplicationLineCommand,
                                   CommandResult<SplitCashApplicationLineCommandResult>>,
                   SplitCashApplicationLineCommandHandler>();

services.AddScoped<IQueryHandler<GetCashApplicationLinesPagedQuery,
                                 GetCashApplicationLinesPagedQueryResult>,
                   GetCashApplicationLinesPagedQueryHandler>();

services.AddScoped<ICashApplicationReviewViewService, CashApplicationReviewViewService>();
```

*Trade-off:* verbose. *Benefit:* every handler is greppable, every dependency is explicit, "find usages" works.

---

# Recommended lifetimes

The rule of thumb that's saved every Blazor Server team from itself.

| Service | Lifetime | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `IDbContextFactory<T>` | Singleton | Thread-safe factory |
| `IUnitOfWork` | Scoped | One transactional scope per request/circuit |
| Repositories | Scoped | Stateless data-access entry points |
| Command handlers | Scoped | Request-scoped use-case execution |
| Query handlers | Scoped | Request-scoped reads |
| View services | Scoped | UI-facing orchestration |
| Application services | Scoped | Composition scope |

**Never use a long-lived `DbContext` in Blazor Server.** Always `IDbContextFactory` + short-lived contexts.

