# Authorization model

The most over-engineered part of the codebase. On purpose.

---

# Permission-based authorization

Roles compose permissions. Permissions belong to feature areas with access levels. Policies enforce them.

- **Entities:** `Role` → `RolePermission` → `Permission` → `FeatureArea` + `AccessLevel`
- **`UserRole`** binds a `Party` to a `Role` with optional hierarchy scoping (Region / Market / PnL / CostCenter)
- **`PermissionHandler`** evaluates `PermissionRequirement` against the user's permissions + hierarchy
- **`RegisterFeaturePolicies`** auto-registers `_Global`, `_Regional`, `_Hierarchy` variants of every named policy

*Net: an organization can model "global VCP admin" and "northeast-region SIF approver" with the same machinery.*

---

# Policy naming convention

Predictable. Greppable. Documented in `AuthorizationPolicies.cs`.

- **`{Feature}.CanViewOrEdit`** — combined: global OR regional access
- **`{Feature}.CanViewOrEdit_Global`** — global-scope only
- **`{Feature}.CanViewOrEdit_Regional`** — region-hierarchy required
- **`{Feature}.CanViewOrEdit_Hierarchy`** — any-hierarchy (for portal)
- **`{Feature}.CanEdit_*`** — edit-only equivalents

*Net: see a policy in code, you can predict its registration shape.*

